#import "template.typ": *

#note(
  title: "Abstract Algebra"
)[
  = Group
  == Group and Subgroup
  #def(name:"Group")[
    Let $G$ be a nonempty set with a binary operation (the operation is closed). If
    - $(a b) c = a (b c)$ for all $a, b, c in G$;
    - there exists $e in G$ such that $e a = a = a e$ for all $a in G$;
    - for every $a in G$, there exists $b in G$ such that $b a = e = a b$;
    then $(G, dot)$ is a group. The element $e$ is the identity, and $b$ is the inverse of $a$, denoted by $a^(-1)$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Semigroup")[
    A nonempty set with an associative binary operation is a semigroup.
  ]
  #def(name:"Abelian Group")[
    A group $(G, dot)$ is abelian if $a b = b a$ for all $a, b in G$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Order")[
    The order of a group $G$ is the number of its elements, denoted by $|G|$.
  ]
  #re[
    - $(a b)^(-1) = b^(-1) a^(-1)$.
    - The identity of a group is unique, and the inverse of each element is unique.
    - After a definition, one should check that it is well-defined.
  ]
  #def(name:"Left Identity")[
    Let $G$ be a semigroup. An element $e_l in G$ is a left identity if $e_l g = g$ for every $g in G$. An element $a_l^(-1)$ is a left inverse of $a$ if $a_l^(-1) a = e_l$.
  ]
  #prop[
    A semigroup $G$ is a group if and only if it has a left identity and every element has a left inverse.
  ]
  #pf[
    The forward direction is immediate. Conversely, let $e$ be a left identity, and for each $a$ let $a_l^(-1)$ satisfy $a_l^(-1) a = e$. The left inverse of $a_l^(-1)$ then gives
    $ a a_l^(-1) = e (a a_l^(-1)) = ((a_l^(-1))_l^(-1) a_l^(-1)) (a a_l^(-1)) = (a_l^(-1))_l^(-1) (a_l^(-1) a) a_l^(-1) = (a_l^(-1))_l^(-1) a_l^(-1) = e. $
    Thus $a_l^(-1)$ is also a right inverse. Moreover
    $ a e = a a_l^(-1) a = e a = a, $
    so $e$ is a right identity, and $G$ is a group.
  ]
  #prop[
    A finite semigroup $G$ is a group if and only if it satisfies the left and the right cancellation laws.
  ]
  #pf[
    The forward direction is trivial. Conversely, left cancellation makes $L_a : x |-> a x$ injective, hence bijective. Choose $e_l$ with $a e_l = a$. For every $b$,
    $ a e_l b = a b, $
    so left cancellation yields $e_l b = b$. Thus $e_l$ is a left identity. Likewise $R_a : x |-> x a$ is bijective, so each $a$ has a left inverse relative to $e_l$. The previous proposition shows that $G$ is a group.
  ]
  #def(name:"Subgroup")[
    Let $(G, dot)$ be a group. A nonempty subset $H subset.eq G$ is a subgroup of $G$ if $(H, dot)$ is a group, denoted by $H <= G$.
  ]
  #re[
    A subgroup has the same identity as the original group.
  ]
  #prop[
    Let $H$ be a nonempty subset of a group $G$. Then $H <= G$ if and only if $a b^(-1) in H$ whenever $a, b in H$.
  ]
  #pf[
    If $H$ is a subgroup, then $a, b in H$ gives $b^(-1) in H$ and $a b^(-1) in H$. Conversely, take $a = b$ to get $e = a a^(-1) in H$. Take $a = e$ to get $b^(-1) in H$. Replacing $b$ by $b^(-1)$ then gives $a b in H$.
  ]
  #re[
    If $H <= G$, then $H^(-1) = H = H H$.
  ]
  #prop[
    Let $A, B <= G$. Then $A B <= G$ if and only if $A B = B A$.
  ]
  #pf[
    If $A B$ is a subgroup, then $A B = (A B)^(-1) = B^(-1) A^(-1) = B A$. Conversely, assume $A B = B A$. Since $A$ and $B$ are subgroups,
    $ A B (A B)^(-1) = A B B^(-1) A^(-1) = A B B A = A B A = B A A = B A = A B. $
    Thus $A B (A B)^(-1) = A B$, so $A B <= G$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Center")[
    The center of a group $G$ is
    $ C(G) = {x in G | forall g in G, x g = g x}. $
  ]
  #def(name:"Centralizer")[
    For $a in G$, the centralizer of $a$ is $C_G (a) = {g in G | a g = g a}$. For $H subset.eq G$,
    $ C_G (H) = {g in G | forall h in H, g h = h g}. $
  ]
  #def(name:"Normalizer")[
    For $H subset.eq G$, the normalizer of $H$ is $N_G (H) = {g in G | g H = H g}$.
  ]
  #re[
    $C(G)$, $C_G (a)$, $C_G (H)$, and $N_G (H)$ are all subgroups of $G$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Subgroup Generated by a Set")[
    Let $M$ be a nonempty subset of $G$, and let $T = {H <= G | M subset.eq H}$. Then
    $ chevron.l M chevron.r = inter.big_(H in T) H $
    is the smallest subgroup of $G$ containing $M$, called the subgroup generated by $M$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Cyclic Group")[
    If $chevron.l M chevron.r = chevron.l a chevron.r$ for a single element $a$, then $chevron.l a chevron.r$ is the cyclic group generated by $a$.
  ]
  #eg[
    $(ZZ, +) = chevron.l 1 chevron.r$ and $(ZZ_n, +) = chevron.l overline(1) chevron.r$.\
    The symmetric group $S_3$ has ${(1 2), (1 3)}$ as a generating set.\
    Every generating set of $S_n$ has at least $n - 1$ elements.
  ]
  == Coset and Conjugacy
  #def(name:"Equivalence Relation")[
    A relation $R$ on a nonempty set $S$ is an equivalence relation if it is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.
  ]
  #def(name:"Coset")[
    Let $H <= G$ and $a in G$. The sets
    $ a H = {a h | h in H}, quad H a = {h a | h in H} $
    are the left coset and the right coset of $H$ represented by $a$.
  ]
  #re[
    - A coset is a subgroup only in the case $a in H$.
    - Distinct elements of $G$ may determine the same coset.
    - $a H = b H$ if and only if $a^(-1) b in H$.
    - Either $a H = b H$ or $a H inter b H = emptyset$.
    - $|a H| = |b H|$.
    - A left coset need not be a right coset.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Lagrange")[
    If $H <= G$ and $G$ is finite, then $|H| [G : H] = |G|$, where the index $[G : H]$ is the number of left cosets of $H$.
  ]
  #prop(name:"Product Formula")[
    Let $A, B <= G$, with $A$ and $B$ finite. Then
    $ |A B| = (|A| |B|) / (|A inter B|). $
  ]
  #def(name:"Order of an Element")[
    The order of $a in G$ is the smallest positive integer $m$ such that $a^m = e$. If no such $m$ exists, one sets $o(a) = oo$.
  ]
  #re[
    Let $a, b in G$.
    - If $o(a) < oo$, then $o(a) divides |G|$.
    - If $a^t = e$, then $o(a) divides t$.
    - If $o(a) = m < oo$, $o(b) = n < oo$, $a b = b a$, and $gcd(m, n) = 1$, then $o(a b) = m n$.
  ]
  #re[
    - $chevron.l a chevron.r = chevron.l a^(-1) chevron.r$.
    - If $G$ is finite, then $G = chevron.l a chevron.r$ if and only if $|G| = o(a)$.
    - A cyclic group of order $n$ has $phi(n)$ generators.
    - Every subgroup of a cyclic group is cyclic.
  ]
  #cor[
    A group of prime order is cyclic, hence abelian.
  ]
  #def(name:"Conjugacy")[
    Elements $a, b in G$ are conjugate if $b = g^(-1) a g$ for some $g in G$. The equivalence class of $a$ is the conjugacy class $[a]$.
  ]
  #thm[
    $ abs([a]) = [G : C_G (a)]. $
  ]
  #thm(name:"Class Equation")[
    Let $R$ be a set of representatives of the conjugacy classes of $G$. Then
    $ abs(G) = abs(C(G)) + sum_(a in R \\ C(G)) frac(abs(G), abs(C_G (a))). $
  ]
  #def(name: [$p$-Group])[
    A group of order $p^n$, with $p$ prime and $n >= 1$, is a $p$-group.
  ]
  #thm[
    A $p$-group has nontrivial center.
  ]
  #pf[
    If $|C(G)| = 1$, the class equation gives
    $ sum_(a in R \\ C(G)) frac(abs(G), abs(C_G (a))) = p^n - 1. $
    Each index $frac(abs(G), abs(C_G (a)))$ is a positive power of $p$, so the left side is divisible by $p$, while $p^n - 1$ is not.
  ]
  #cor[
    A group of order $p^2$ is abelian.
  ]
  #pf[
    The center is nontrivial, so $|C(G)|$ is $p$ or $p^2$. If $|C(G)| = p$ and $a in.not C(G)$, then $C(G) subset.eq C_G (a)$ and the containment is proper, hence $|C_G (a)| = p^2$. Thus $a in C(G)$, a contradiction. Therefore $C(G) = G$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Conjugate Subset")[
    For $A subset.eq G$ and $g in G$, the set $g^(-1) A g$ is a conjugate of $A$.
  ]
  #thm[
    The number of distinct conjugates of a subset $A subset.eq G$ is $[G : N_G (A)]$.
  ]
  == Group Homomorphism
  #def(name:"Homomorphism")[
    A map $f : G -> H$ is a group homomorphism if $f(a b) = f(a) f(b)$ for all $a, b in G$. It is a monomorphism if it is injective, an epimorphism if it is surjective, and an isomorphism if it is bijective.
  ]
  #def(name:"Isomorphic Groups")[
    If there exists an isomorphism $f : G -> H$, one denotes $G tilde.equiv H$.
  ]
  #thm[
    An infinite cyclic group is isomorphic to $(ZZ, +)$, and a cyclic group of order $n$ is isomorphic to $(ZZ_n, +)$.
  ]
  #pf[
    Let $G = chevron.l a chevron.r$. The map $pi : (ZZ, +) -> G$ given by $m |-> a^m$ is a surjective homomorphism, because $pi(m + n) = a^(m + n) = pi(m) pi(n)$. It is injective when $G$ is infinite. When $|G| = n$, it induces an isomorphism $overline(m) |-> a^m$ from $ZZ_n$ onto $G$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Normal Subgroup")[
    A subgroup $H <= G$ is normal, denoted by $H triangle.l G$, if $g^(-1) h g in H$ for all $h in H$ and $g in G$.
  ]
  #lem[
    For $H <= G$, the following are equivalent.
    - $H triangle.l G$.
    - $g^(-1) H g subset.eq H$ for every $g in G$.
    - $g^(-1) H g = H$ for every $g in G$.
    - $g H = H g$ for every $g in G$.
    - The collection of left cosets equals the collection of right cosets.
  ]
  #re[
    Normality is not transitive: $H_1 triangle.l G$ and $H_2 triangle.l H_1$ do not imply $H_2 triangle.l G$.
  ]
  #re[
    If $a H = a' H$ and $b H = b' H$, then $a^(-1) a', b^(-1) b' in H$, hence
    $ (a b)^(-1) (a' b') = (b^(-1) (a^(-1) a') b) (b^(-1) b') in H. $
    Thus $(a b) H = (a' b') H$. Associativity is inherited from $G$. Since $H b = b H$, the same product is the setwise product $(a H)(b H) = a (H b) H = (a b) H$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Quotient Group")[
    If $H triangle.l G$, the set $G slash H$ of left cosets is a group under the operation $(a H)(b H) = (a b) H$. The identity is $H$, the inverse of $a H$ is $a^(-1) H$, and one denotes $overline(g) = g H$.
  ]
  #prop[
    A subgroup of index $2$ is normal.
  ]
  #def(name:"Kernel and Image")[
    For a homomorphism $f : G -> tilde(G)$,
    $ ker f = {x in G | f(x) = e}, quad op("Im") f = {f(x) | x in G}. $
  ]
  #re[
    $ker f triangle.l G$ and $op("Im") f <= tilde(G)$.
  ]
  #eg[
    The homomorphisms $f : ZZ_12 -> ZZ_18$ are determined by $f(overline(1)) = overline(a)$, where $12 overline(a) = overline(0)$. Thus $o(overline(a))$ divides $gcd(12, 18) = 6$, so the possible images of $overline(1)$ are
    $ overline(0), overline(9), overline(6), overline(12), overline(3), overline(15). $
  ]
  #thm(name:"First Isomorphism Theorem")[
    If $f : G -> tilde(G)$ is a homomorphism, then $op("Im") f tilde.equiv G slash ker f$ by $f(x) |-> x ker f$. Equivalently, if $g : G -> K$ is a homomorphism with $g(ker f) = {e}$, then there is a unique homomorphism $tilde(g) : G slash ker f -> K$ such that $tilde(g) compose pi = g$, where $pi : G -> G slash ker f$ is the quotient map.
  ]
  #def(name:"Canonical Homomorphism")[
    If $N triangle.l G$, the canonical homomorphism is $pi : G -> G slash N$, $x |-> x N$.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Correspondence Theorem")[
    Let $N triangle.l G$. The map $H |-> H slash N$ is a bijection between the subgroups of $G$ containing $N$ and the subgroups of $G slash N$, and it preserves normality.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Second Isomorphism Theorem")[
    Let $N triangle.l G$ and $H <= G$. Then $H N = N H <= G$, $H inter N triangle.l H$, and
    $ H N slash N tilde.equiv H slash (H inter N). $
  ]
  #thm(name:"Third Isomorphism Theorem")[
    Let $A triangle.l G$, $B triangle.l G$, and $A <= B$. Then $B slash A triangle.l G slash A$, and
    $ (G slash A) slash (B slash A) tilde.equiv G slash B. $
  ]
  #def(name:"Permutation Group")[
    Elements of $S_n$ are permutations, and subgroups of $S_n$ are permutation groups. For an arbitrary set $Omega$, subgroups of $S(Omega)$ are transformation groups.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Cayley")[
    Every group is isomorphic to a transformation group. Every finite group is isomorphic to a permutation group.
  ]
  == Permutation and Group Action
  #def(name:"Even and Odd Permutations")[
    A permutation is even if it is a product of an even number of transpositions, and odd otherwise.
  ]
  #def(name:"Alternating Group")[
    $A_n$ is the set of all even permutations in $S_n$. Then $A_n <= S_n$ and $abs(A_n) = frac(abs(S_n), 2)$.
  ]
  #re[
    The index $[S_n : A_n] = 2$, so $A_n triangle.l S_n$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Group Action")[
    A group $G$ acts on a set $Omega$ if there is a homomorphism $rho : G -> S(Omega)$. The kernel of the action is
    $ ker rho = {g in G | rho(g) = id_Omega}. $
    The action is faithful if $ker rho = {e}$.
  ]
  #def(name: [$G$-Set])[
    A set $Omega$ is a $G$-set if there is a map $G times Omega -> Omega$, $(g, x) |-> g x$, such that $e x = x$ and $(g h) x = g (h x)$ for all $x in Omega$ and $g, h in G$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Orbit and Stabilizer")[
    The orbit and the stabilizer of $x in Omega$ are
    $ O_x = {g x | g in G}, quad G_x = {g in G | g x = x}. $
    The stabilizer is a subgroup of $G$. The action is transitive if $Omega$ has only one orbit.
  ]
  #re[
    For $x, y in Omega$, either $O_x = O_y$ or $O_x inter O_y = emptyset$. Thus $Omega$ is the disjoint union of the orbits.
  ]
  #lem[
    The map $O_x -> G slash G_x$, $g x |-> g G_x$, is a bijection.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Orbit-Stabilizer")[
    If $G$ acts on $Omega$, then
    $ abs(O_x) = [G : G_x] = frac(abs(G), abs(G_x)) $
    whenever the orbit is finite. If the action is transitive, then $abs(Omega) = frac(abs(G), abs(G_x))$.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Burnside")[
    Let a finite group $G$ act on a finite set $Omega$, and let $t$ be the number of orbits. If $F_g = |{x in Omega | g x = x}|$, then
    $ t = 1 / (|G|) sum_(g in G) F_g. $
  ]
  #eg[
    How many necklaces can be made from six beads, two of each of three colors, up to rotation and reflection?
  ]
  #sol[
    The symmetry group is the dihedral group $D_6$ of order $12$. The identity fixes $6! / (2!)^3 = 90$ colorings. The rotations by $1$, $2$, $4$, and $5$ positions fix none, the rotation by $3$ positions fixes $6$, and each of the six reflections fixes $6$. Hence
    $ t = 1 / 12 (90 + 6 + 6 dot 6) = 11. $
  ]
  == Sylow Theorem
  #def(name:"Simple Group")[
    A group $G != {e}$ is simple if its only normal subgroups are ${e}$ and $G$.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Sylow I")[
    Let $G$ be a finite group, $p$ a prime, and $r >= 1$. If $p^r$ divides $|G|$, and $N(p^r)$ denotes the number of subgroups of order $p^r$, then
    $ N(p^r) equiv 1 (mod p). $
    In particular, $G$ has a subgroup of order $p^r$.
  ]
  #def(name: [Sylow $p$-Subgroup])[
    Let $|G| = p^m n$ with $p$ prime, $m >= 1$, and $p$ not dividing $n$. A subgroup of order $p^m$ is a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Sylow II")[
    Any two Sylow $p$-subgroups of a finite group are conjugate.
  ]
  #re[
    If $P$ is a Sylow $p$-subgroup, then
    $ N(p^m) = k p + 1 = frac(abs(G), abs(N_G (P))). $
    In particular, $k = 0$ means $P triangle.l G$, since every conjugate of $P$ is again a Sylow $p$-subgroup.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Sylow III")[
    Every $p$-subgroup of a finite group is contained in some Sylow $p$-subgroup.
  ]
  #eg[
    A group $G$ of order $35$ is cyclic.
  ]
  #pf[
    Indeed $N(5) = 5 k + 1$ divides $7$, and $N(7) = 7 k' + 1$ divides $5$, so there is a unique Sylow $5$-subgroup $H = chevron.l a chevron.r$ and a unique Sylow $7$-subgroup $K = chevron.l b chevron.r$. Both are normal, and $H inter K = {e}$, so
    $ a^(-1) b^(-1) a b in H inter K. $
    Thus $a b = b a$. The element $a b$ has order $35$, so $G = chevron.l a b chevron.r$.
  ]
  #eg[
    No group of order $148 = 2^2 dot 37$ is simple.
  ]
  #pf[
    Here $N(37) = 37 k + 1$ divides $4$, so $N(37) = 1$ and the Sylow $37$-subgroup is normal.
  ]
  #eg[
    No group of order $56 = 2^3 dot 7$ is simple.
  ]
  #pf[
    The possibilities are $N(7) divides 8$ with $N(7) equiv 1 (mod 7)$, and $N(8) divides 7$ with $N(8) equiv 1 (mod 2)$, so $N(7) in {1, 8}$ and $N(8) in {1, 7}$. These two larger values cannot occur together. If $N(7) = 1$, the Sylow $7$-subgroup is normal. If $N(7) = 8$, the eight Sylow $7$-subgroups meet pairwise at ${e}$ and cover $6 dot 8 + 1 = 49$ elements. The remaining $56 - 49 = 7$ elements, together with $e$, form a unique Sylow $2$-subgroup, which is normal.
  ]
  #eg[
    A group of order $6$ is isomorphic to $ZZ_6$ or to $S_3$.
  ]
  #pf[
    Here $N(3) = 1$, while $N(2)$ is $1$ or $3$. If $N(2) = 1$, take $o(a) = 3$ and $o(b) = 2$. Both Sylow subgroups are normal and intersect trivially, so $a b = b a$ and $o(a b) = 6$. Thus $G tilde.equiv ZZ_6$. If $N(2) = 3$, conjugation on the three Sylow $2$-subgroups gives a homomorphism $f : G -> S_3$. The action is transitive, so $ker f != G$. A kernel of order $2$ would be a normal Sylow $2$-subgroup, and a kernel of order $3$ would normalize every Sylow $2$-subgroup, forcing one of them to be normal. Either case contradicts $N(2) = 3$. Hence $f$ is an isomorphism and $G tilde.equiv S_3$.
  ]
  #eg[
    For a prime $p$ and $m >= 2$, no group of order $p^m$ is simple.
  ]
  #pf[
    The class equation gives $|C(G)| != 1$, and $C(G) triangle.l G$. If the center is proper it is a nontrivial proper normal subgroup. If $C(G) = G$, then $G$ is abelian and any subgroup of order $p$ is a nontrivial proper normal subgroup.
  ]
  #eg[
    If $p$ and $q$ are distinct primes, no group of order $p q$ is simple.
  ]
  #pf[
    Assume $p < q$. Then $N(q) equiv 1 (mod q)$ and $N(q)$ divides $p$, so $N(q) = 1$.
  ]
  #eg[
    If $p$ and $q$ are distinct primes, no group of order $p^2 q$ is simple.
  ]
  #pf[
    One has $N(p^2) = k p + 1$ dividing $q$, and $N(q) = k' q + 1$ dividing $p^2$. If $p > q$, then $N(p^2) = 1$. If $p < q$, then $N(q)$ cannot be $p$, because $p equiv 1 (mod q)$ is impossible, so $N(q)$ is $1$ or $p^2$. In the latter case the Sylow $q$-subgroups contribute $p^2 (q - 1) + 1$ elements, and the remaining $p^2 - 1$ non-identity elements form a unique subgroup of order $p^2$. Thus $N(p^2) = 1$.
  ]
  #eg[
    No group of order $24$ is simple.
  ]
  #pf[
    Let $G$ act by conjugation on its Sylow $3$-subgroups. If there is only one, it is normal. If there are four, the action gives a homomorphism $f : G -> S_4$. The kernel is a proper normal subgroup. It is nontrivial, or else $f$ is injective and $G tilde.equiv S_4$, which is not simple.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Frattini")[
    Let $N triangle.l G$, and let $P$ be a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $N$. Then $G = N N_G (P)$.
  ]
  #pf[
    For $g in G$ one has $g P g^(-1) <= N$. This conjugate is a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $N$, so $n P n^(-1) = g P g^(-1)$ for some $n in N$. Then $g^(-1) n in N_G (P)$, and hence $g in N N_G (P)$.
  ]
  == Finitely Generated Abelian Group
  #def(name:"External Direct Product")[
    Let $G_1$ and $G_2$ be groups. The set $G_1 times G_2$ becomes a group under
    $ (a_1, b_1) (a_2, b_2) = (a_1 a_2, b_1 b_2). $
    It is the external direct product, denoted by $G_1 times G_2$. If the groups are additive, one denotes $G_1 plus.o G_2$.
  ]
  #re[
    Set $tilde(G)_1 = G_1 times {e_2}$ and $tilde(G)_2 = {e_1} times G_2$. Then $tilde(G)_i tilde.equiv G_i$, $tilde(G)_i triangle.l G_1 times G_2$, and $tilde(G)_1 tilde(G)_2 = G_1 times G_2$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Internal Direct Product")[
    Let $H, K triangle.l G$. If $G = H K$ and $H inter K = {e}$, then $G$ is the internal direct product of $H$ and $K$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Quaternion Group")[
    The quaternion group is
    $ Q_8 = {plus.minus e, plus.minus i, plus.minus j, plus.minus k}, $
    with $i^2 = j^2 = k^2 = -e$, $i j = k = -j i$, $j k = i = -k j$, and $k i = j = -i k$.
  ]
  #re[
    As matrices,
    $ Q_8 tilde.equiv {plus.minus mat(1, 0; 0, 1), plus.minus mat(0, 1; -1, 0), plus.minus mat(i, 0; 0, -i), plus.minus mat(0, i; i, 0)}. $
  ]
  #def(name:"Free Abelian Group")[
    The group $ZZ^n$ is the free abelian group of rank $n$. If a finitely generated free abelian group $G$ has a basis of $r$ elements, then $r$ is the rank of $G$.
  ]
  #eg[
    $ chevron.l a, b | a^n = 1, b^2 = 1, b a = a^(-1) b chevron.r = D_n, \
      chevron.l a, b | a^4 = 1, a^2 = b^2, b a = a^3 b chevron.r = Q_8. $
    Here $D_n$ is the dihedral group of order $2 n$.
  ]
  #lem[
    Let $G$ be a finite abelian group of order $n = p_1^(m_1) dots.c p_t^(m_t)$. Then
    $ G = G_1 plus.o dots.c plus.o G_t, $
    where $G_i$ is the Sylow $p_i$-subgroup of $G$.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Fundamental Theorem of Finite Abelian Groups")[
    Let $G$ be an abelian group of order $n = p_1^(m_1) dots.c p_t^(m_t)$. Write each $m_i = m_(i 1) + dots.c + m_(i k_i)$ with positive integers $m_(i j)$. Then
    $ G tilde.equiv plus.o_(i = 1)^t (ZZ_(p_i^(m_(i 1))) plus.o dots.c plus.o ZZ_(p_i^(m_(i k_i)))). $
    The prime-power orders $p_i^(m_(i j))$ are the elementary divisors of $G$. Equivalently, there exist positive integers $d_1 divides d_2 divides dots.c divides d_s$ with $d_1 dots.c d_s = n$ such that
    $ G tilde.equiv ZZ_(d_1) plus.o dots.c plus.o ZZ_(d_s). $
    The integers $d_1, dots, d_s$ are the invariant factors of $G$.
  ]
  #eg[
    How many abelian groups of order $900$ are there up to isomorphism?
  ]
  #sol[
    Write $900 = 2^2 dot 3^2 dot 5^2$. An abelian group of order $p^2$ is isomorphic to $ZZ_(p^2)$ or to $ZZ_p plus.o ZZ_p$, so there are $2^3 = 8$ isomorphism types. By elementary divisors, and the corresponding invariant factors, they are
    $
      ZZ_4 plus.o ZZ_9 plus.o ZZ_25 tilde.equiv ZZ_900, \
      ZZ_4 plus.o ZZ_9 plus.o ZZ_5 plus.o ZZ_5 tilde.equiv ZZ_5 plus.o ZZ_180, \
      ZZ_4 plus.o ZZ_3 plus.o ZZ_3 plus.o ZZ_25 tilde.equiv ZZ_3 plus.o ZZ_300, \
      ZZ_4 plus.o ZZ_3 plus.o ZZ_3 plus.o ZZ_5 plus.o ZZ_5 tilde.equiv ZZ_15 plus.o ZZ_60, \
      ZZ_2 plus.o ZZ_2 plus.o ZZ_9 plus.o ZZ_25 tilde.equiv ZZ_2 plus.o ZZ_450, \
      ZZ_2 plus.o ZZ_2 plus.o ZZ_9 plus.o ZZ_5 plus.o ZZ_5 tilde.equiv ZZ_10 plus.o ZZ_90, \
      ZZ_2 plus.o ZZ_2 plus.o ZZ_3 plus.o ZZ_3 plus.o ZZ_25 tilde.equiv ZZ_6 plus.o ZZ_150, \
      ZZ_2 plus.o ZZ_2 plus.o ZZ_3 plus.o ZZ_3 plus.o ZZ_5 plus.o ZZ_5 tilde.equiv ZZ_30 plus.o ZZ_30.
    $
  ]
  = Ring
  == Ring Basics
  #def(name:"Ring")[
    A ring is a set $R$ with two binary operations $+$ and $dot$ such that
    - $(R, +)$ is an abelian group, whose identity $0$ is the zero element;
    - $(R, dot)$ is a semigroup;
    - multiplication distributes over addition on both sides, namely $a (b + c) = a b + a c$ and $(b + c) a = b a + c a$.
    If multiplication is commutative, $R$ is a commutative ring. The additive inverse of $a$ is written $-a$.
  ]
  #eg[
    The polynomial ring is $R[x] = {a_n x^n + dots.c + a_1 x + a_0 | a_i in R}$.
  ]
  #prop[
    In a ring, $0 a = 0 = a 0$, $(-a) b = -a b = a (-b)$, and $(n a) b = n (a b) = a (n b)$. An identity element, if it exists, is unique.
  ]
  #def(name:"Zero Divisor")[
    A nonzero element $a$ is a left zero divisor if $a b = 0$ for some $b != 0$, and a right zero divisor if $b a = 0$ for some $b != 0$. Both are called zero divisors.
  ]
  #prop[
    Cancellation holds in a ring without zero divisors: $a b = a c, a != 0$ implies $b = c$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Integral Domain")[
    An integral domain is a commutative ring with identity and with no zero divisors.
  ]
  #def(name:"Unit")[
    Let $R$ be a nonzero ring with identity. An element with a multiplicative inverse is a unit of $R$. The set of all units is $U(R)$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Division Ring and Field")[
    A nonzero ring $R$ with identity is a division ring if $U(R) = R \\ {0}$.\
    A commutative division ring is a field. 
  ]
  #def(name:"Subring")[
    A nonempty subset $S$ of a ring $R$ is a subring if $S$ with the operations of $R$ is a ring.
  ]
  #prop[
    A nonempty subset $S$ is a subring if and only if $a - b in S$ and $a b in S$ for all $a, b in S$.
  ]
  #prop[
    A subset $F$ of a field $K$ is a subfield if and only if $a - b in F$ and $a b^(-1) in F$ whenever $a, b in F$ and $b != 0$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Ring Homomorphism")[
    A map $f : R -> R'$ is a ring homomorphism if $f(a + b) = f(a) + f(b)$ and $f(a b) = f(a) f(b)$ for all $a, b in R$.
  ]
  #re[
    A ring homomorphism need not send the identity to the identity.
  ]
  == Ideal and Quotient Ring
  #def(name:"Ideal")[
    Let $I$ be a subgroup of $(R, +)$. If $r a in I$ for all $a in I$ and $r in R$, then $I$ is a left ideal. If $a r in I$, then $I$ is a right ideal. An ideal, denoted by $I triangle.l R$, means a two-sided ideal.
  ]
  #re[
    An ideal is a subring. If $I_1$ and $I_2$ are ideals, then so are $I_1 inter I_2$, $I_1 + I_2$, and
    $ I_1 I_2 = {sum_(i = 1)^n r_i s_i | r_i in I_1, s_i in I_2}. $
  ]
  #def(name:"Principal Ideal and Principal Ideal Domain")[
    An ideal generated by one element is a principal ideal. An integral domain in which every ideal is principal is a principal ideal domain (PID).
  ]
  #def(name:"Quotient Ring")[
    If $I triangle.l R$, the quotient group $R slash I$ is a ring under the multiplication $(a + I)(b + I) = a b + I$.
  ]
  #eg[
    $ QQ[x] slash chevron.l x^2 - 2 chevron.r tilde.equiv QQ[sqrt(2)] = {a + b sqrt(2) | a, b in QQ}. $
    The map $a + b x + chevron.l x^2 - 2 chevron.r |-> a + b sqrt(2)$ is a well-defined bijection, and it is the isomorphism induced by the evaluation homomorphism $QQ[x] -> QQ[sqrt(2)]$, whose kernel is $chevron.l x^2 - 2 chevron.r$.
  ]
  #thm(name:"First Isomorphism Theorem")[
    If $f : R -> R'$ is a ring homomorphism, then $ker f triangle.l R$ and $op("Im") f tilde.equiv R slash ker f$.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Second and Third Isomorphism Theorems")[
    Let $I triangle.l R$ and let $S$ be a subring of $R$. Then $I inter S triangle.l S$, $I triangle.l I + S$, and
    $ (I + S) slash I tilde.equiv S slash (I inter S). $
    If also $J triangle.l R$ and $I subset.eq J$, then $J slash I triangle.l R slash I$ and
    $ (R slash I) slash (J slash I) tilde.equiv R slash J. $
  ]
  #def(name:"Simple Ring")[
    A ring whose only ideals are ${0}$ and itself is a simple ring.
  ]
  #def(name:"Prime Ideal")[
    A proper ideal $P$ of $R$ is prime if, whenever $I$ and $J$ are ideals with $I J subset.eq P$, one has $I subset.eq P$ or $J subset.eq P$.
  ]
  #re[
    Let $R$ be a commutative ring with identity, and let $P$ be a proper ideal. The following are equivalent.
    - $P$ is prime.
    - Whenever $a b in P$, one has $a in P$ or $b in P$.
    - $R slash P$ is an integral domain.
  ]
  #def(name:"Maximal Ideal")[
    A proper ideal $M$ is maximal if $M subset.eq I triangle.l R$ implies $I = M$ or $I = R$.
  ]
  #prop[
    Let $R$ be a commutative ring with identity, and let $P$ be an ideal. Then $P$ is maximal if and only if $R slash P$ is a field. In particular, a maximal ideal is prime.
  ]
  #prop[
    In a nonzero ring with identity, every proper ideal is contained in a maximal ideal.
  ]
  #def(name:"Characteristic")[
    If there is a positive integer $m$ such that $m r = 0$ for every $r in R$, the smallest such $m$ is the characteristic of $R$, denoted by $op("char") R$. Otherwise $op("char") R = 0$.
  ]
  #prop[
    In a ring with identity and without zero divisors, every nonzero element has the same additive order. That order is either infinite or prime, and it equals $op("char") R = o(1)$.
  ]
  #pf[
    Let $e$ be the identity and $n = o(e)$. For $r != 0$, if $m r = 0$, then $(m e) r = 0$, so $m e = 0$ and $n divides m$. Also $n r = (n e) r = 0$, so the additive order of $r$ divides $n$. Thus every nonzero element has additive order $n$. If $n = s t$, then $(s e)(t e) = n e = 0$, so $s e = 0$ or $t e = 0$. Hence $n$ is prime or infinite.
  ]
  #prop[
    If $R$ has an identity $e$, then $phi : ZZ -> R$, $n |-> n e$, is a ring homomorphism.
  ]
  #re[
    If $op("char") R = 0$, then $R$ contains a subring isomorphic to $ZZ$.
  ]
  #prop(name:"Frobenius")[
    Let $R$ be a commutative ring of prime characteristic $p$. Then $f : R -> R$, $r |-> r^p$, is a ring homomorphism.
  ]
  #def(name:"Field of Fractions")[
    Let $D$ be an integral domain and $D^* = D \\ {0}$. On $D times D^*$, declare $(r, s) ~ (r', s')$ when $r s' = r' s$. The equivalence classes, written $r slash s$, form a field $K$ under the usual addition and multiplication of fractions. This field is the field of fractions of $D$.
  ]
  #re[
    The field of fractions of $ZZ$ is $QQ$. The field of fractions of $R[x]$ is
    $ R(x) = {f slash g | f, g in R[x], g != 0}. $
    In particular $QQ(sqrt(2)) = QQ[sqrt(2)]$.
  ]
  #prop[
    A field of characteristic $0$ contains a subfield isomorphic to $QQ$. A field of characteristic $p > 0$ contains a subfield isomorphic to $ZZ_p$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Direct Sum of Rings")[
    On $R_1 times dots.c times R_n$ one defines
    $ (a_1, dots, a_n) + (b_1, dots, b_n) = (a_1 + b_1, dots, a_n + b_n), $
    and likewise for multiplication, coordinate by coordinate. The resulting ring is the direct sum $R_1 plus.o dots.c plus.o R_n$.
  ]
  #prop[
    $R tilde.equiv R_1 plus.o dots.c plus.o R_n$ if and only if $R$ has ideals $I_i tilde.equiv R_i$ such that every element of $R$ can be written uniquely as $r = r_1 + dots.c + r_n$ with $r_i in I_i$. One then says that $R$ is the internal direct sum of $I_1, dots, I_n$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Comaximal Ideals")[
    Ideals $I$ and $J$ are comaximal if $I + J = R$.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Chinese Remainder Theorem")[
    Let $I_1, dots, I_n$ be pairwise comaximal ideals of a ring $R$ with identity. Then
    $ R slash (I_1 inter dots.c inter I_n) tilde.equiv R slash I_1 plus.o dots.c plus.o R slash I_n, $
    by $r + (I_1 inter dots.c inter I_n) |-> (r + I_1, dots, r + I_n)$.
  ]
  #eg[
    The maximal ideals of $ZZ_8 plus.o ZZ_15$ are $2 ZZ_8 plus.o ZZ_15$, $ZZ_8 plus.o 3 ZZ_15$, and $ZZ_8 plus.o 5 ZZ_15$.
  ]
  == Unique Factorization
  #def(name:"Divisibility")[
    Let $D$ be an integral domain and $a, b in D$. If $a = b c$ for some $c in D$, then $b$ divides $a$, written $b divides a$. If $b divides a$ and $a divides b$, then $a$ and $b$ are associates, written $a ~ b$.
  ]
  #re[
    $b divides a$ if and only if $chevron.l a chevron.r subset.eq chevron.l b chevron.r$, and $a ~ b$ if and only if $chevron.l a chevron.r = chevron.l b chevron.r$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Irreducible Element")[
    A nonzero nonunit $p in D$ is irreducible if $p = a b$ implies that $a$ is a unit or $b$ is a unit. Equivalently, $b divides p$ implies $b in U(D)$ or $b ~ p$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Prime Element")[
    A nonzero nonunit $p in D$ is prime if $p divides a b$ implies $p divides a$ or $p divides b$.
  ]
  #lem[
    In an integral domain, every prime element is irreducible.
  ]
  #def(name:"Norm")[
    For $a + b sqrt(d) in QQ[sqrt(d)]$, the norm is $N(a + b sqrt(d)) = |a^2 - d b^2|$.
  ]
  #lem[
    Let $D$ be an integral domain and $p in D \\ {0}$. Then $p$ is prime if and only if $chevron.l p chevron.r$ is a prime ideal.
  ]
  #def(name:"Unique Factorization Domain")[
    An integral domain $D$ is a unique factorization domain (UFD) if every nonzero nonunit $a in D$ can be written as a product of irreducible elements, and the factorization is unique in the following sense. If $a = p_1 dots.c p_n = q_1 dots.c q_m$ with each factor irreducible, then $n = m$ and, after reordering, $p_i ~ q_i$ for every $i$.
  ]
  #thm[
    In a UFD, every irreducible element is prime.
  ]
  #def(name:"Factor Chain")[
    A sequence $a_1, a_2, dots$ in an integral domain is a factor chain if each $a_i$ is a proper divisor of $a_(i - 1)$, for $i >= 2$.
  ]
  #thm[
    Every factor chain in a UFD is finite.
  ]
  #lem[
    An integral domain is a UFD if every nonzero nonunit has a finite factor chain and every irreducible element is prime.
  ]
  #def(name:"Standard Factorization")[
    In a UFD, every nonzero $a$ may be written $a = eta p_1^(r_1) dots.c p_s^(r_s)$, where $eta$ is a unit, the $p_i$ are pairwise nonassociate irreducible elements, and each $r_i$ is a positive integer.
  ]
  #def(name:"Greatest Common Divisor")[
    Let $a, b in D$ be nonzero. An element $d$ is a greatest common divisor of $a$ and $b$ if $d divides a$, $d divides b$, and every common divisor of $a$ and $b$ divides $d$.
  ]
  #lem[
    If every pair of elements in an integral domain has a greatest common divisor, then every irreducible element is prime.
  ]
  #thm[
    In a UFD, every pair of elements has a greatest common divisor.
  ]
  #prop[
    Every factor chain in a PID is finite.
  ]
  #prop[
    Let $D$ be a PID and let $a in D \\ {0}$. The following are equivalent.
    - $a$ is prime.
    - $a$ is irreducible.
    - $chevron.l a chevron.r$ is a maximal ideal.
    - $chevron.l a chevron.r$ is a prime ideal.
  ]
  #thm[
    Every PID is a UFD.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Gauss")[
    If $D$ is a UFD, then so is $D[x]$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Indeterminate")[
    Let $R$ be a ring with identity, and let $tilde(R)$ be a ring containing $R$ as a subring. An element $x in tilde(R)$ is an indeterminate over $R$ if $r x = x r$ for every $r in R$, and whenever $a_0, dots, a_n in R$ are not all zero,
    $ a_0 + a_1 x + dots.c + a_n x^n != 0. $
  ]
  #prop[
    Every commutative ring with identity admits an indeterminate. The resulting polynomial ring is $R[x]$.
  ]
  #cor(name:"Remainder Theorem")[
    Let $R$ be a commutative ring with identity, $f in R[x]$, and $c in R$. There is a unique $q in R[x]$ such that $f(x) = q(x) (x - c) + f(c)$.
  ]
  #cor[
    Over an integral domain, a polynomial of degree $n$ has at most $n$ roots.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Eisenstein")[
    Let $D$ be a UFD and let $f(x) = a_n x^n + dots.c + a_1 x + a_0 in D[x]$ have content $1$, that is $gcd(a_0, dots, a_n) = 1$. If a prime element $p in D$ divides $a_i$ for every $i < n$, does not divide $a_n$, and $p^2$ does not divide $a_0$, then $f$ is irreducible in $D[x]$.
  ]
  #re[
    $ZZ_2 [x] slash chevron.l x^3 + x + 1 chevron.r$ and $ZZ_2 [x] slash chevron.l x^3 + x^2 + 1 chevron.r$ are fields with eight elements.
  ]
  #def(name:"Euclidean Domain")[
    An integral domain $D$ is a Euclidean domain if there is a function $phi : D -> NN$ such that $phi(x) = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$, and for all $a, b in D$ with $b != 0$ there exist $q, r in D$ with
    $ a = q b + r, quad phi(r) < phi(b). $
  ]
  #prop[
    Every Euclidean domain is a PID, and hence a UFD.
  ]
  #re[
    The classes of rings satisfy
    $ "field" subset.neq "Euclidean domain" subset.neq "PID" subset.neq "UFD" subset.neq "integral domain". $
  ]
  = Field
  == Field Extension
  #def(name:"Field Extension")[
    If $F$ is a subfield of a field $K$, then $K$ is an extension of $F$, denoted by $K slash F$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Algebraic and Transcendental Elements")[
    An element $u$ of some extension of $F$ is algebraic over $F$ if it is a root of a nonzero polynomial in $F[x]$, and transcendental over $F$ otherwise. The extension $K slash F$ is algebraic if every element of $K$ is algebraic over $F$, and transcendental otherwise.
  ]
  #def(name:"Minimal Polynomial")[
    Let $u$ be algebraic over $F$. The monic polynomial in $F[x]$ of least degree having $u$ as a root is the minimal polynomial of $u$ over $F$.
  ]
  #prop[
    Let $alpha$ be algebraic over $F$, and let $u(x) in F[x]$ be monic. The following are equivalent.
    - $u$ is the minimal polynomial of $alpha$.
    - $u$ is irreducible over $F$ and $u(alpha) = 0$.
    - Whenever $f(alpha) = 0$, one has $u divides f$.
  ]
  #prop[
    Let $K slash F$ and $u in K$, and write $F[u] = {f(u) | f in F[x]}$. If $u$ is algebraic over $F$ with minimal polynomial $f$, then
    $ F[u] = F(u) tilde.equiv F[x] slash chevron.l f chevron.r. $
    If $u$ is transcendental over $F$, then $F[u] tilde.equiv F[x]$.
  ]
  #thm(name:"Tower Law")[
    If $F subset.eq K subset.eq E$ are fields and the degrees are finite, then $[E : F] = [E : K] [K : F]$.
  ]
  #cor[
    Let $u$ and $v$ lie in some extension of $F$.
    - If both are transcendental over $F$, then there is an isomorphism $sigma : F(u) -> F(v)$ with $sigma(u) = v$ and $sigma bar.v_F = id$.
    - If both are algebraic over $F$, then such an isomorphism exists if and only if $u$ and $v$ have the same minimal polynomial over $F$.
  ]
  #thm[
    Let $f in F[x]$ have degree $n >= 1$. There is a simple extension $K = F(u)$ such that
    - $u in K$ is a root of $f$;
    - $[F(u) : F] <= n$, and equality holds if and only if $f$ is irreducible in $F[x]$;
    - if $f$ is irreducible, then $K$ is unique up to an isomorphism that fixes $F$ and sends the adjoined root to any prescribed root.
  ]
  #def(name:"Algebraically Closed Field")[
    A field $K$ is algebraically closed if every element that is algebraic over $K$, in any extension of $K$, already lies in $K$.
  ]
  == Splitting Field
  #def(name:"Galois Group")[
    For an extension $K slash F$,
    $ op("Gal")(K slash F) = {sigma in op("Aut")(K) | sigma bar.v_F = id}. $
  ]
  #lem[
    Let $u$ be algebraic over $F$, with minimal polynomial $f$. The order $|op("Gal")(F(u) slash F)|$ equals the number of distinct roots of $f$ that lie in $F(u)$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Splitting Field")[
    Let $f in F[x]$ have positive degree. An extension $E slash F$ is a splitting field of $f$ over $F$ if
    $ f(x) = c (x - a_1) dots.c (x - a_n) $
    with $c in F$ and $a_i in E$, and $E = F(a_1, dots, a_n)$. Thus $E$ is the smallest extension of $F$ containing every root of $f$.
  ]
  #lem[
    If $f in F[x]$ is irreducible, there is a simple extension $F(u)$, unique up to an isomorphism fixing $F$, such that $u$ is a root of $f$.
  ]
  #prop[
    Every polynomial of positive degree over $F$ has a splitting field over $F$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Separable Polynomial")[
    A polynomial $f in F[x]$ of positive degree is separable if every irreducible factor of $f$ has no repeated root in a splitting field over $F$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Formal Derivative")[
    The formal derivative of $f(x) = a_0 + a_1 x + dots.c + a_n x^n$ is
    $ f'(x) = a_1 + 2 a_2 x + dots.c + n a_n x^(n - 1). $
  ]
  #lem[
    A polynomial $f in F[x]$ of positive degree has a repeated root if and only if $gcd(f, f') != 1$. This is a property of $f$ itself and does not depend on the extension of $F$ in which the roots are taken.
  ]
  #thm[
    Let $sigma : F -> F'$ be an isomorphism of fields, let $f in F[x]$ have positive degree, and let $E$ and $E'$ be splitting fields of $f$ over $F$ and of $sigma(f)$ over $F'$ respectively. Then $sigma$ extends to an isomorphism $E -> E'$. The number $m$ of such extensions satisfies $1 <= m <= [E : F]$, and $m = [E : F]$ if and only if $f$ is separable.
  ]
  #cor[
    If $E$ is a splitting field of $f$ over $F$, then $|op("Gal")(E slash F)| <= [E : F]$.
  ]
  #eg[
    The splitting field of $x^5 - 1$ over $QQ$ is the fifth cyclotomic field $E = QQ(zeta)$, where $zeta = e^(2 pi i slash 5)$.
  ]
  #re[
    Each $sigma_k in op("Gal")(E slash QQ)$ is determined by $sigma_k (zeta) = zeta^k$ with $gcd(k, 5) = 1$. The map $sigma_k |-> k$ is an isomorphism
    $ op("Gal")(E slash QQ) tilde.equiv (ZZ_5)^times tilde.equiv ZZ_4. $
  ]
  == Finite Field
  #def(name:"Finite Field")[
    A field with finitely many elements is a finite field, also called a Galois field.
  ]
  #thm[
    Let $F$ be a finite field. Then $op("char") F = p$ for some prime $p$, $|F| = p^n$ where $n = [F : ZZ_p]$, and $(F, +) tilde.equiv ZZ_p^n$.
  ]
  #thm[
    The multiplicative group of a finite field $F$ with $p^n$ elements is cyclic of order $p^n - 1$. Thus $F = ZZ_p (u)$ for some algebraic element $u$ of degree $n$ over $ZZ_p$.
  ]
  #def(name:"Primitive Element")[
    A generator of the simple extension $F slash ZZ_p$ may be taken to be a generator of $F^times$. Such an element is a primitive element.
  ]
  #thm[
    For every prime $p$ and every integer $n >= 1$, there exists a field of order $p^n$, and it is unique up to isomorphism.
  ]
  #def(name:"Frobenius Automorphism")[
    On a field $F$ of order $p^n$, the map $sigma_p : F -> F$, $a |-> a^p$, is an automorphism of $F$, called the Frobenius automorphism.
  ]
  #re[
    The subfields of a field of order $p^n$ are exactly the fields of order $p^m$ with $m divides n$.
  ]
]
